Then the math becomes music

This is where the metaphor stops being merely metaphorical.

Musical consonance genuinely has an intimate relationship with small integer ratios.

An octave is:

2:1

A perfect fifth:

3:2

A major third:

5:4

A harmonic seventh is approximately:

7:4

So the first few primes already form a harmonic vocabulary.

Now reinterpret our semantic primes acoustically.

If 2 = distinction, an octave can represent same identity, new level.

If 3 = relation, a fifth can represent strong coupling.

If 5 = transformation, the major third can inject color.

If 7 = deviation, the seventh introduces a stranger, less domesticated harmonic relationship.

Suddenly a semantic object can become a chord.

Suppose:

\text{journey}=2\cdot3\cdot5

Instead of reading that only as a number, play the harmonic relationships associated with 2,3,5.

You have turned an abstraction into an audible object.

And then you can do something better:

play the delta.

State A:

2\cdot3\cdot5

State B:

2\cdot3\cdot7

The shared factors 2,3 remain sounding.

Remove 5.

Introduce 7.

Now the listener hears exactly what changed.

That’s Delta Calculus as harmony.

⸻

There is another beautiful operation.

Take several primes as clocks:

2,\ 3,\ 5,\ 7

Trigger a sound every 2 beats, another every 3, another every 5, another every 7.

They continually separate and recombine.

Their complete alignment repeats after:

\operatorname{LCM}(2,3,5,7)=210

beats.

So LCM becomes reunion time.

GCD tells us what two structures already share.

LCM tells us how large a cycle is required before their different periodicities completely reconcile.

That pair is almost philosophically perfect:

\boxed{\text{GCD = common ancestry}}

\boxed{\text{LCM = future convergence}}

And now you can see why primes are such interesting “actors”: relatively prime cycles refuse to synchronize cheaply. They generate long structures before repetition.

That is useful in music, procedural animation, game design, scheduling, cryptography, pattern generation, and—more abstractly—our semantic web.

⸻

And there is one further move I think is genuinely worth keeping.

Don’t assign primes to words permanently.

Assign them to operators.

Because current meaning electricity, water, and now all share flow.

Root in plants, algebra, linguistics, and harmony shares generation/origin.

Bridge shares connection across separation.

The vocabulary can change while the factor remains invariant.

So beneath language you’d gradually build something like a periodic table of semantic operations.

Words become molecules.

Sentences become reactions.

Ideas become compounds.

Reasoning becomes chemistry.

Sequences become music.

And deltas become transformations of the factorization.

That is a much stronger system than collecting clever puns, because now the junctions become cross-domain measurement instruments.